<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=mercator-projection">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mercator projection</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1569">1569</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mercator projection is a way of drawing the globe on a flat map in which courses of constant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a> bearing, rhumb lines, appear as straight lines, so that a chart serves sailors directly. Gerardus Mercator introduced it on his world map of 1569, printed from 18 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> plates he engraved himself, by increasing the degrees of latitude towards each pole as the parallels lengthen. He left no account of his method, and Edward Wright and others later showed how to calculate the latitude scale exactly. Acceptance was slow and came only with the French sea atlas Le <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neptune">Neptune</a> Francois at the end of the seventeenth century; the projection is still the basis of navigation charts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mercator-projection.jpg" alt="Mercator projection" width="330" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerardus Mercator · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mercator_1569_world_map_composite.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
